# Otto local environment — copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in.
# `run.sh` auto-loads `.env`; values are exported to the worker + server.
# These are all OPTIONAL — Otto runs without them.

# --- secret provider (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------
# By default every secret below lives in plaintext here. Set a helper command instead and Otto
# resolves them from your password manager: env (this file) -> helper -> unset. Only the secrets
# still EMPTY here are looked up, so a value set below always wins.
#
# `{name}` is replaced with the variable name (OTTO_SLACK_USER_TOKEN, …); a command without it
# gets the name appended. Parsed with shlex, NOT run through a shell — for a pipeline, say so:
#   OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=sh -c 'op read op://otto/{name} | tr -d "\n"'
# Only the first line of stdout is used. A non-zero exit, empty output, or a prompt that outlives
# OTTO_SECRET_TIMEOUT_S all read as "unset" — never an error, so check `python3 doctor.py`.
#
#   pass:      OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=pass show otto/{name}
#   1Password: OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=op read op://otto/{name}/credential
#   Bitwarden: OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=bw get password otto/{name}
#   KeePassXC: OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=keepassxc-cli show -sa password ~/otto.kdbx {name}
#   gpg:       OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=gpg --quiet --decrypt /home/you/.otto-secrets/{name}.gpg
#
# This one is env-ONLY and never editable from Admin: the web API is unauthenticated by design,
# and a shell command settable over HTTP is a shell command an attacker can set.
# OTTO_SECRET_COMMAND=
# Hard ceiling on the helper, seconds (default 3). A locked vault agent PROMPTS; without this
# the import that resolved the secret would hang the worker at startup.
# OTTO_SECRET_TIMEOUT_S=3

# Enables the event/webhook ingress (POST /api/events/<source>). Until this is set, the
# endpoint returns 503. It's the HMAC-SHA256 key senders sign the request body with
# (X-Otto-Signature header). Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
OTTO_EVENT_SECRET=

# Timezone for scheduled-job cron expressions (defaults to the server's local zone).
# OTTO_SCHEDULE_TZ=Pacific/Auckland

# NOTE: the knobs marked [Admin] below are also editable in the UI (Admin → Runtime settings,
# persisted to data/settings.json). A value set HERE wins over the UI — that's deliberate, so a
# headless/systemd deployment can pin behaviour — and the UI shows it as env-pinned/locked.
# Leave one commented out if you want to drive it from the UI.

# [Admin] Max verify->retry attempts before giving up (default 3).
# OTTO_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# [Admin] Claude fallback for LOCAL models (default 1 = on). On: whenever a local model fails, times out,
# is marked down, returns nothing usable, or can't drive tools, Otto continues the work on Claude
# so the run still lands. Set to 0 for STRICT LOCAL mode: that substitution becomes illegal, the
# local failure IS the outcome (loud reason in the result, audit and Blocked column, zero Claude
# tokens), and a verify-failed local run stays local instead of escalating to Claude. Use it to
# find out whether local is really carrying the work — with fallback on, a dead local endpoint
# looks like a run of successes. The `verify` judge is exempt either way: it's the guard that
# catches a bad local execution, so it must stay available.
# OTTO_LOCAL_FALLBACK=0

# LLM supervisor (issue #143), SHADOW mode: mid-run checkpoints on the "supervise" model tier
# record whether it WOULD have retried the attempt — it never touches the run. On by default;
# a checkpoint only fires on long attempts (>=OTTO_SUPERVISOR_EVERY_S seconds AND
# >=OTTO_SUPERVISOR_MIN_EVENTS activity events since the last one), so short runs cost nothing.
# OTTO_SUPERVISE=1
# OTTO_SUPERVISOR_EVERY_S=120
# OTTO_SUPERVISOR_MIN_EVENTS=4
# OTTO_SUPERVISOR_MAX_CHECKS=10

# Push notifications (issue #92): set a topic to get an ntfy push (phone app / browser at
# https://ntfy.sh/<topic>) whenever a run blocks on you — awaiting approval, awaiting
# clarification, or ended needs-human. The topic name is effectively a SECRET (anyone who
# knows it can read/post): pick something unguessable, e.g.  openssl rand -hex 12
# OTTO_NTFY_TOPIC=
# Self-hosted ntfy server (default https://ntfy.sh).
# OTTO_NTFY_URL=
# Also push (low priority) when an UNATTENDED run finishes CLEANLY, not just when one blocks on
# you. Off by default. Interactive chat turns never push on completion whatever this is set to —
# the answer is already on the screen you are looking at.
# OTTO_NTFY_ON_COMPLETE=true
# Where tapping a notification lands; `#run=<id>` is appended so the tap opens THAT run. The
# default (http://localhost:$PORT) is a dead link on the phone the push went to — set this to
# the tunnel URL once cloudflared ingress (issue #41) is up.
# OTTO_CLICK_URL=
# Approve/Deny buttons on the approval push, so a parked run clears with one tap. Needs
# OTTO_CLICK_URL reachable from the phone. OFF by default: each button carries a single-use
# token for that ONE run, but it rides on the broker, so it is only as private as the topic name.
# OTTO_NTFY_ACTIONS=true
# How long an action token stays redeemable (default 86400 = the gate's own deadline).
# OTTO_NTFY_ACTION_TTL_S=86400
# Drop a push identical to one sent this many seconds ago (default 120). Only exists to swallow
# a duplicate from a retry — a re-previewed plan re-pushes the same title minutes later.
# OTTO_NTFY_DEDUPE_S=120
# Include the REQUEST TEXT in the push. OFF by default: ntfy is a third-party broker (messages
# are cached server-side and the topic name is the only credential), so a push carries what
# happened and where to look — capability, risk, repo, source, run id — and the content stays on
# your machine, one tap away in the UI. Turn this on only for a self-hosted, access-controlled
# ntfy server. Credential-shaped strings are scrubbed either way.
# OTTO_NTFY_DETAIL=true
# How much of the request survives when OTTO_NTFY_DETAIL is on (chars).
# OTTO_NTFY_DETAIL_CHARS=300

# Slack auto-answer listener (Events tab -> "Slack auto-answer" toggle): when set AND enabled in
# the UI, Otto polls Slack AS YOU and answers allowlisted DMs / @-mentions on your behalf.
# This is a Slack USER OAuth token (xoxp-...), NOT a bot token. Create a Slack app, add these
# USER token scopes, install it to your workspace, and paste the user token here:
#   im:history im:read mpim:history channels:history groups:history chat:write users:read search:read
# Feature is OFF until this is set AND you enable + add an allowlist in the Events tab.
# OTTO_SLACK_USER_TOKEN=

# Auto-discovers the cloud model list only; execution always uses your Claude subscription
# via `claude -p`, so this is NOT required.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
